---
/**
 * What GRPO actually optimises: not the reward, but the reward minus the group's own mean.
 *
 * Two groups of eight candidates. The left one has spread -- the rewards come from
 * `rukh.train.rewards` over eight plausible moves in one position -- and its baseline sits where
 * the mean is, so four candidates get pushed up and four get pushed down. The right one is flat:
 * every sample is the same move, every reward is the same number, the baseline lands on top of
 * all of them and every advantage is zero. The engine was paid for both.
 *
 * That second group is not a hypothetical. It is the reason `GrpoResult` carries `flat_groups`
 * separately from `groups`: the count says how much of the budget the model's own confidence is
 * throwing away, and there is no way to know it in advance.
 *
 * The dashed line settles onto the group's own mean on a loop, because the subtraction is the one
 * step readers reconstruct wrongly: they expect the tallest bar to win, and what wins is the bar
 * furthest from the line. It is the only animated figure of the module -- the other five compare
 * measured numbers, where motion would be decoration rather than explanation.
 */
const left = [0.98, 0.74, 1.21, 0.31, 0.62, 0.05, 0.88, 0.45];
const right = [0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74, 0.74];

const mean = (values: number[]) => values.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / values.length;

const bar = (value: number) => Math.max(2, value * 74);
const columns = (values: number[], originX: number) =>
  values.map((value, index) => ({ value, x: originX + index * 26 }));

const panels = [
  {
    title: 'un grupo con dispersión',
    values: left,
    originX: 46,
    verdict: 'ocho ventajas, cuatro arriba y cuatro abajo',
  },
  {
    title: 'un grupo que se repite',
    values: right,
    originX: 366,
    verdict: 'ocho ceros: el motor ya está pagado',
  },
];
---

<figure class="fig fig--group">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 640 250"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Dos grupos de ocho candidatas. En el de la izquierda las recompensas van de 0,05 a 1,21 y la media cae en 0,655: cuatro candidatas quedan por encima y cuatro por debajo, así que cuatro se empujan hacia arriba y cuatro hacia abajo. En el de la derecha las ocho valen 0,74, la media cae encima de todas y las ocho ventajas son cero, aunque el motor se haya pagado igual."
  >
    <g font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--muted)">
      {panels.map((panel) => {
        const baseline = mean(panel.values);
        return (
          <g>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="24" fill="var(--ink)">
              {panel.title}
            </text>
            {columns(panel.values, panel.originX).map((column) => (
              <g>
                <rect
                  x={column.x}
                  y={160 - bar(column.value)}
                  width="16"
                  height={bar(column.value)}
                  rx="1.5"
                  fill={column.value >= baseline ? 'var(--accent)' : 'var(--diagram-stroke)'}
                  fill-opacity={column.value >= baseline ? '0.34' : '0.2'}
                  stroke={column.value >= baseline ? 'var(--accent)' : 'var(--line)'}
                  stroke-width="0.8"
                />
              </g>
            ))}
            <g class="fig-group__baseline">
              <line
                x1={panel.originX - 8}
                y1={160 - bar(baseline)}
                x2={panel.originX + 8 * 26}
                y2={160 - bar(baseline)}
                stroke="var(--accent)"
                stroke-width="1.2"
                stroke-dasharray="4 3"
              ></line>
              <text
                x={panel.originX + 8 * 26 + 4}
                y={160 - bar(baseline) + 3}
                font-size="9"
                fill="var(--accent-text-aa)"
              >
                {baseline.toFixed(2)}
              </text>
            </g>
            <line
              x1={panel.originX - 8}
              y1="160"
              x2={panel.originX + 8 * 26}
              y2="160"
              stroke="var(--line)"
              stroke-width="0.8"
            ></line>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="182" font-size="9">
              recompensa
            </text>
            <text x={panel.originX - 36} y="206" font-size="9" fill="var(--accent-text-aa)">
              {panel.verdict}
            </text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
      <text x="46" y="236" font-size="9">
        la línea a trazos es la media del propio grupo: no hay red de valor que entrenar, y lo que
        se empuja es la distancia a ella
      </text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="caption">
    GRPO no optimiza la recompensa: optimiza la recompensa menos la media de su propio grupo. Un
    grupo que se repite entero tiene esa media encima de todo y no aporta un solo gradiente.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
